= 1
{parent=Mathematics IV}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Write $\times$ for the paper's $\wedge$. In a right-handed <Cartesian coordinate system>, the <cross product> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a\times\mathbf b=(a_2b_3-a_3b_2,\ a_3b_1-a_1b_3,\ a_1b_2-a_2b_1).}
$$
It is perpendicular to both <vectors>, has <norm> $|\mathbf a||\mathbf b|\sin\phi$, where $\phi$ is their <angle>, and has direction given by the <right-hand rule> when nonzero. Its <norm> is the <area> of the <parallelogram> spanned by the two <vectors>.

If $\mathbf b\times\mathbf c\ne0$, use that <parallelogram> as the base of the <parallelepiped>. Its perpendicular height is $|\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf n|$, where $\mathbf n=(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)/|\mathbf b\times\mathbf c|$. Thus its <volume> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal V=|\mathbf b\times\mathbf c|\,|\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf n|=|\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)|.}
$$
If $\mathbf b\times\mathbf c=0$, both the <volume> and this <scalar triple product> vanish. If the <cross product> is nonzero, the <scalar triple product> vanishes exactly when $\mathbf a$ lies in the <plane> spanned by $\mathbf b,\mathbf c$. If it is zero, $\mathbf b,\mathbf c$ are <linearly dependent>, and adjoining $\mathbf a$ still spans at most a <plane>. Consequently **the three <vectors> are coplanar if and only if $\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)=0$**, including cases involving a zero <vector>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Expand the first component using the definition of the <cross product>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)]_1
&=a_2(b_1c_2-b_2c_1)-a_3(b_3c_1-b_1c_3)\\
&=b_1(a_2c_2+a_3c_3)-c_1(a_2b_2+a_3b_3)\\
&=b_1(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-c_1(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b).
\end{aligned}
$$
The added $a_1b_1c_1$ terms cancel in the last step. Cyclically permuting the component indices gives the other two components, proving the <vector triple product identity>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)=\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-\mathbf c(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b).}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Put $V=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)$. The <vector triple product identity> gives
$$
(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\times(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)
=\mathbf c[(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf a]-\mathbf a[(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf c]
=V\mathbf c.
$$
Taking its <dot product> with $\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$ and using cyclic invariance of the <scalar triple product> yields
$$
\boxed{(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\cdot[(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\times(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)]=V^2.}
$$
There was no division by $V$, so this also proves the identity for coplanar <vectors>.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $V=\mathbf a\cdot(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\ne0$. The <scalar triple product> is invariant under a cyclic permutation, so the definitions of the <reciprocal basis> give
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a'\cdot\mathbf a=\frac VV=1,\qquad\mathbf b'\cdot\mathbf b=\frac VV=1,\qquad\mathbf c'\cdot\mathbf c=\frac VV=1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <cross product> $\mathbf b\times\mathbf c$ is perpendicular to both factors. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a'\cdot\mathbf b=\frac{(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf b}{V}=0,\qquad\mathbf a'\cdot\mathbf c=\frac{(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\cdot\mathbf c}{V}=0.}
$$
Cyclic permutations give all the off-diagonal <dot products> between the original <basis> and its <reciprocal basis>.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Apply the <squared scalar triple product from cyclic cross products> proved in <1/c>:
$$
\mathbf a'\cdot(\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c')
=\frac{(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)\cdot[(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)\times(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)]}{V^3}
=\frac{V^2}{V^3}.
$$
Hence the <reciprocal basis> has <scalar triple product> $\boxed{\mathbf a'\cdot(\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c')=1/V}$, with its sign retained.

= iv
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Since $V\ne0$, <1/d/iii> gives $\mathbf a'\cdot(\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c')=1/V\ne0$. The criterion in <1/a> therefore proves that **$\mathbf a',\mathbf b',\mathbf c'$ are non-coplanar**. In particular they form a <basis> of $\mathbb R^3$.

= v
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

The <vector triple product identity> gives
$$
\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c'
=\frac{(\mathbf c\times\mathbf a)\times(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)}{V^2}
=\frac{V\mathbf a}{V^2}=\frac{\mathbf a}{V}.
$$
Eliminate $V$ with <1/d/iii> to obtain the <reciprocal basis involution>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a=\frac{\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c'}{\mathbf a'\cdot(\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c')}.}
$$
This formula actually holds without the unit-length assumptions. Under those assumptions it also says $|\mathbf b'\times\mathbf c'|=1/|V|$; normalizing this <cross product> alone would lose the sign of $V$ and could return $-\mathbf a$.

= 2
{parent=Mathematics IV}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <quadric surface> $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$ is the unit <sphere> centered at the origin. Each section $z=c$, for $|c|<1$, is a <circle> of <radius> $\sqrt{1-c^2}$.

The <quadric surface> $z=x^2+y^2$ is a circular <elliptic paraboloid>, opening in the positive $z$ direction with vertex at the origin. Its horizontal sections $z=c>0$ are <circles> of <radius> $\sqrt c$, and its vertical sections through the $z$ axis are <parabolas>.

For $ax^2+y^2=0$, $z$ is unrestricted. If $a>0$, both nonnegative terms must vanish, so the locus is **the $z$ axis**, a <straight line>. If $a=0$, it is **the <plane> $y=0$**, represented by the repeated factor $y^2$. If $a<0$, factorization gives
$$
(y-\sqrt{-a}\,x)(y+\sqrt{-a}\,x)=0,
$$
so the locus is **two distinct <planes>, $y=\pm\sqrt{-a}\,x$, meeting along the $z$ axis**. These are degenerate <quadrics>, not ordinary curved cylinders.

\Image[mathematics-iv-q2-quadrics.png]
{title=The <sphere>, <elliptic paraboloid>, and three degenerate <quadric> cases in Question 2(a)}

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Choose the rotated <Cartesian coordinates>
$$
X=\frac{x+y}{\sqrt2},\qquad Y=\frac{y-x}{\sqrt2},\qquad Z=z.
$$
Their inverse is $x=(X-Y)/\sqrt2$, $y=(X+Y)/\sqrt2$. Consequently $x^2+y^2=X^2+Y^2$ and $xy=(X^2-Y^2)/2$, giving the <canonical form of a quadric>
$$
\boxed{\left(1+\frac a2\right)X^2+\left(1-\frac a2\right)Y^2+Z^2=1.}
$$
The <orthogonal diagonalization> identifies the <eigenvalues> $1+a/2,1-a/2,1$ of the <symmetric matrix> of the <quadratic form>.

For $|a|<2$, all three coefficients are positive: the surface is an <ellipsoid> with semiaxes $(1+a/2)^{-1/2},(1-a/2)^{-1/2},1$. At $a=0$ it is a <sphere>.

For $a=2$, the equation is $2X^2+Z^2=1$, an <elliptic cylinder> parallel to the $Y$ axis. For $a=-2$, it is $2Y^2+Z^2=1$, an <elliptic cylinder> parallel to the $X$ axis. The absent coordinate is unrestricted; its disappearance does not turn the <surface> into an <ellipse>.

For $a>2$, the negative coefficient is that of $Y^2$: this is a <one-sheet hyperboloid> with axis $Y$. For $a<-2$, the negative coefficient is that of $X^2$: it is a <one-sheet hyperboloid> with axis $X$. This gives the <signature transition from an ellipsoid to an elliptic cylinder and a one-sheet hyperboloid>, including both transition values.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Write $N=(n_1,n_2,n_3)$. Restriction to the <plane> $z=0$ gives
$$
M_{11}x^2+(M_{12}+M_{21})xy+M_{22}y^2+n_1x+n_2y+k=0.
$$
This is an equation of <degree> at most two in two <Cartesian coordinates>, the general algebraic form of a <conic>, with possible <degenerate conics> and an <empty set> of real points. No <determinant> hypothesis is needed for this substitution.

For an arbitrary <plane>, choose a point $\mathbf x_0$ and an <orthonormal basis> $\mathbf e,\mathbf f$ of its direction <linear subspace>. Put $B=(\mathbf e\ \mathbf f)$ and $\mathbf x=\mathbf x_0+B\boldsymbol\xi$, where $\boldsymbol\xi=(s,t)^T$. With $S=(M+M^T)/2$, the <symmetric part of a matrix>, the restricted equation is
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\xi^TB^TSB\boldsymbol\xi+(2\mathbf x_0^TSB+NB)\boldsymbol\xi+\mathbf x_0^TS\mathbf x_0+N\mathbf x_0+k=0.}
$$
This proves the plane-section result whenever “<conic>” allows the appropriate degeneracies. A <rigid motion> taking the chosen <plane> to $z=0$ gives the same argument.

**The source's assumption $\det M\ne0$ does not guarantee a nondegenerate <conic>.** Even with $M=I$ and $f=x^2+y^2+z^2-1$, the section $z=1$ is a single <point>, and $z=2$ has no real points. With the nonsingular <symmetric matrix> giving $f=2xz+y^2$, the section $z=0$ is the repeated <straight line> $y=0$.

There is a further literal omission: the source does not assume $M$ is symmetric. For
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&1\\-1&0&1\\1&-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad N=0,\qquad k=0,
$$
one has $\det M=2$ but $f=2xz$. Thus the section $z=0$ is the entire <plane>, rather than a <conic>. Only $S$ controls the <quadratic form>; invertibility of an arbitrary $M$ cannot remove this exception. If the intended hypothesis is that $M$ is symmetric and nonsingular, the quadratic restriction to a <plane> cannot vanish identically: otherwise $B^TMB=0$, so $M$ would map a two-dimensional <linear subspace> injectively into its one-dimensional <orthogonal complement>, which is impossible. Degenerate and empty sections still remain possible.

