= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For a piecewise <continuously differentiable> <parametric curve>, the <arc length> is the integral of <speed>:
$$
\boxed{s=\int_a^b\sqrt{[x'(t)]^2+[y'(t)]^2}\,dt.}
$$
The <Pythagorean theorem> gives the length of an infinitesimal displacement as $\sqrt{dx^2+dy^2}$. Integration counts traversal length, including repeated portions if the <parametrization> retraces them. Piecewise continuous <derivatives>, or suitable <absolute continuity>, justify the formula; mere differentiability as stated in the source is not by itself the usual sufficient regularity hypothesis.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

In <polar coordinates>, $x=r(\theta)\cos\theta$ and $y=r(\theta)\sin\theta$. The <product rule> gives
$$
x'=r'\cos\theta-r\sin\theta,\qquad y'=r'\sin\theta+r\cos\theta.
$$
Squaring and adding cancels the mixed terms and gives $(x')^2+(y')^2=(r')^2+r^2$. Hence the <arc length> is
$$
\boxed{s=\int_{\theta_1}^{\theta_2}\sqrt{r(\theta)^2+\left(\frac{dr}{d\theta}\right)^2}\,d\theta.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For the <cardioid>, $r'=-a\sin\theta$. The expression under the <square root> simplifies to
$$
r^2+(r')^2=a^2(2+2\cos\theta)=4a^2\cos^2(\theta/2).
$$
Since $0\leq\theta\leq\theta_2\leq\pi$, the <cosine> is nonnegative. Therefore
$$
\boxed{s(\theta_2)=\int_0^{\theta_2}2a\cos(\theta/2)\,d\theta=4a\sin(\theta_2/2).}
$$
The upper half of the <cardioid> starts at $(2a,0)$, passes through $(0,a)$, bends to negative $x$, and ends at the cusp $(0,0)$ when $\theta_2=\pi$. Its full upper-half <arc length> is $4a$; reflecting gives $8a$ for the full <cardioid>.

The <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\frac{ds}{d\theta_2}=2a\cos(\theta_2/2).
$$
This decreases from $2a$ to zero, so **the <arc length> increases fastest at $\theta_2=0$**, interpreted as the right-hand rate at the initial endpoint. If only positive endpoints are allowed, the rate has supremum $2a$ as $\theta_2\downarrow0$, rather than an attained interior maximum.

\Image[mathematics-iv-q3-cardioid.png]
{title=A partial <cardioid> with $\theta_2=2\pi/3$, and the continuation to its cusp}

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Parametrize the <function graph> by $\mathbf R(x,y)=(x,y,f(x,y))$. Its two tangent <vectors> are $\mathbf R_x=(1,0,f_x)$ and $\mathbf R_y=(0,1,f_y)$. A small parameter <rectangle> maps, to first order, to a tangent <parallelogram> with <area> $|\mathbf R_x\times\mathbf R_y|\,dx\,dy$. Since
$$
\mathbf R_x\times\mathbf R_y=(-f_x,-f_y,1),
$$
the <surface area of a graph> is
$$
\boxed{A=\iint_S\sqrt{1+f_x^2+f_y^2}\,dx\,dy.}
$$
For example, $f\in C^1$ ensures a <regular surface> chart; more general domains can be handled by the corresponding convergent <improper integral>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

A <parametrization> of the <surface of revolution> is
$$
\mathbf R(x,\phi)=(x,f(x)\cos\phi,f(x)\sin\phi),\qquad a<x<b,\quad0\leq\phi<2\pi.
$$
Its tangent <vectors> are orthogonal, with <norms> $\sqrt{1+f'(x)^2}$ and $|f(x)|$. Thus
$$
\boxed{A=2\pi\int_a^b|f(x)|\sqrt{1+f'(x)^2}\,dx.}
$$
The <absolute value> is necessary if the <function graph> passes below the axis. The formula gives the curved <surface area>; it adds no end discs. Zeros of $f$ can be treated by splitting the integral into regular portions and taking limits.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The rotation axis is $L=\{(x,1,0):x\in\mathbb R\}$. The distance of the generating <function graph> from this axis is $\sqrt x$, so a <parametrization> is
$$
\mathbf R(x,\phi)=(x,1+\sqrt x\cos\phi,\sqrt x\sin\phi),\qquad0<x<1.
$$
The <surface of revolution> therefore has equation $(y-1)^2+z^2=x$: it is a circular <elliptic paraboloid> opening along the positive $x$ direction, with its vertex and its rim at $x=1$ excluded. Translating the axis does not change the <surface area> formula, so
$$
\begin{aligned}
A&=2\pi\int_0^1\sqrt x\sqrt{1+\frac1{4x}}\,dx
=\pi\int_0^1\sqrt{4x+1}\,dx\\
&=\boxed{\frac\pi6(5\sqrt5-1).}
\end{aligned}
$$
The cancellation makes the <improper integral> finite at the vertex. Neither the missing vertex nor the missing boundary <circle> changes the <surface area>, and no cap is included.

\Image[mathematics-iv-q3-revolution.png]
{title=The <elliptic paraboloid> obtained by rotating $y=1+\sqrt x$ around $L$}

= 4
{parent=Mathematics IV}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Divide <Newton's second law> by the <mass> and resolve along the radial and transverse unit <vectors> in <polar coordinates>. A <central force> has no transverse component, so
$$
\ddot r-r\dot\theta^2=-F(r),\qquad2\dot r\dot\theta+r\ddot\theta=0.
$$
Multiplication of the second equation by $r$ shows that $d(r^2\dot\theta)/dt=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\ddot r-r\dot\theta^2=-F(r),\qquad r^2\dot\theta=h,}
$$
where $h$ is the signed <specific angular momentum> perpendicular to the <orbital plane>.

If $h\ne0$ and $r>0$, the <polar angle> is locally a valid independent variable. For $u=1/r$, use primes for <derivatives> with respect to $\theta$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\dot\theta=hu^2,\qquad\dot r=-hu',\qquad\ddot r=-h^2u^2u'',\qquad r\dot\theta^2=h^2u^3.
$$
Substitution into the radial equation gives the <Binet equation>
$$
\boxed{u''+u=\frac{F(1/u)}{h^2u^2}.}
$$
If $h=0$, the <orbit> is radial and division by $h$ is unavailable; the first radial equation still applies with $\dot\theta=0$ away from the origin.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The initial transverse <velocity> gives $h=a(L/a)=L$. From $\dot r=-hu'$, the <initial conditions> are $u(0)=1/a$ and $u'(0)=1/a$. Define
$$
\nu=\sqrt{1+L^{-2}}>1.
$$
The <Binet equation> becomes the constant-coefficient <linear ordinary differential equation>
$$
u''+\nu^2u=\frac1{aL^2}.
$$
Its <particular solution> is $(\nu^2-1)/(a\nu^2)$. Applying the two <initial conditions> to the <complementary solution> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac1r=\frac{\nu^2-1+\cos(\nu\theta)+\nu\sin(\nu\theta)}{a\nu^2}.}
$$
The physical branch is the connected range containing $\theta=0$ on which the right-hand side is positive. A zero corresponds to escape to infinite <radius>, rather than continuation to negative $r$.

The source calls $F$ an inward force magnitude, but here $F(r)=(r-a)/(ar^3)$ changes sign at $r=a$. The stated <initial condition> has inward radial <velocity>, so the particle immediately enters $r<a$, where the <force> is outward. The consistent interpretation is that $F$ is the **signed inward radial component** of the <force> per unit <mass>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The oscillatory numerator $\cos(\nu\theta)+\nu\sin(\nu\theta)$ has <amplitude> $\sqrt{1+\nu^2}$. Therefore the reciprocal <radius> stays bounded away from zero exactly when
$$
\nu^2-1>\sqrt{1+\nu^2}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\nu^2>3
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad L^2<\frac12.
$$
In this case the radial bounds are
$$
\frac{a\nu^2}{\nu^2-1+\sqrt{1+\nu^2}}
\leq r\leq
\frac{a\nu^2}{\nu^2-1-\sqrt{1+\nu^2}}.
$$
For completeness, the <potential energy> per unit <mass> is $\Phi=-1/(ar)+1/(2r^2)$, and <conservation of mechanical energy> gives
$$
\frac12\dot r^2+U(r)=\mathcal E,\qquad
U(r)=\frac{1+L^2}{2r^2}-\frac1{ar},\qquad
\mathcal E=\frac{L^2-1/2}{a^2}.
$$
This <effective potential> diverges positively at zero, has one minimum at $r=a(1+L^2)$, and tends to zero from below at infinity. Since the initial radial <velocity> is negative, the particle first reaches an inner <turning point>. If $L^2\geq1/2$, the subsequent outward motion has no finite outer <turning point> and escapes. At equality the escape <speed> tends to zero, but the <radius> is still unbounded; infinity is approached only as time tends to infinity. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{The path is bounded exactly for }0<L<1/\sqrt2.}
$$
The minimum of $U$ permits a <circular orbit> for other initial data, but the nonzero initial radial <velocity> here excludes that exceptional solution.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

With $L=1/\sqrt{n^2-1}$, one has $\nu=n$. Since $n\geq3>\sqrt3$, <4/b/ii> proves boundedness. Write the positive <rational number> $n=p/q$ in lowest terms. The <radius> and its angular <derivative> repeat after $\Delta\theta=2\pi/n$. After $p$ such radial oscillations,
$$
p\Delta\theta=2\pi q,
$$
so both the <position> and the radial and transverse <velocities> return to their initial values. The elapsed time is
$$
T=\frac1L\int_0^{2\pi q}r(\theta)^2\,d\theta,
$$
which is finite and positive because $r$ is bounded above and below by positive constants. Thus **the motion is periodic**. The <orbit> is a closed, generally self-intersecting <rosette orbit>, with $p$ radial oscillations during $q$ complete revolutions; for integral $n$ it has $n$ evenly spaced lobes around the origin. It never reaches the origin and is not a <circular orbit>.

= 5
{parent=Mathematics IV}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Let $\mathbf N$ be the <normal reaction> of the stationary smooth <surface of revolution>. Its normal lies in the span of $\mathbf e_r,\mathbf e_z$, so it has no $\mathbf e_\theta$ component. The transverse component of <Newton's second law>, $m\ddot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf N-mg\mathbf e_z$, is therefore
$$
2\dot r\dot\theta+r\ddot\theta=0,
$$
which gives $\boxed{h=r^2\dot\theta=\text{constant}}$.

The <velocity> is tangent to the stationary <surface>, so $\mathbf N\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}=0$. Take the <dot product> of <Newton's second law> with $\dot{\mathbf r}$:
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(\frac12m|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2\right)=-mg\dot z.
$$
Using $z=f(r)$ proves <conservation of mechanical energy> per unit <mass>:
$$
\boxed{E=\frac12|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2+gf(r)=\text{constant}.}
$$
The ideal <constraint> does no <work>. These equations describe motion while the particle remains constrained to the <surface>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Take $a>0$ and the intended horizontal launch around a parallel of the <surface of revolution>: $\dot r(0)=\dot z(0)=0$, $a\dot\theta(0)=v$. Then $h=av$ and $E=v^2/2+gf(a)$. Since $\dot z=f'(r)\dot r$, the conserved <energy> becomes
$$
\frac12[1+f'(r)^2]\dot r^2+U(r)=E,\qquad
U(r)=\frac{a^2v^2}{2r^2}+gf(r).
$$
Here $U$ is the <radial effective potential on a surface of revolution>. Rearrangement gives
$$
\boxed{D(r):=\frac{v^2}{2g}(r^2-a^2)+r^2[f(a)-f(r)]
=\frac{r^2}{2g}[1+f'(r)^2]\dot r^2\geq0.}
$$
For a nonconstant bounded radial motion with $v>0$, there must be another finite radial bound $b\ne a$ satisfying $D(b)=0$, equivalently
$$
\boxed{\frac{v^2}{g}=\frac{2b^2[f(b)-f(a)]}{b^2-a^2}.}
$$
The interval between the radial bounds must be dynamically accessible: $D\geq0$ inside, and a simple endpoint must have a forbidden region $D<0$ beyond it. Equivalently the motion must lie in a bounded part of $\{r>0:U(r)\leq E\}$ separated from escape by a potential barrier. When $f$ stays finite near zero and $v>0$, the <centrifugal barrier> already excludes $r=0$; an outer barrier is the additional requirement. An arbitrary second algebraic root on a disconnected allowed branch would not establish boundedness.

The initial radial <acceleration>, obtained by projecting <Newton's second law> along $\mathbf e_r+f'(a)\mathbf e_z$, is
$$
\ddot r(0)=\frac{v^2/a-gf'(a)}{1+f'(a)^2}.
$$
Thus $v^2/g>af'(a)$ starts the particle outward, and $v^2/g<af'(a)$ starts it inward. Equality gives the exact <circular orbit> $r=a$ and needs no second distinct radial bound. A double root at a different radius can instead be approached asymptotically: this <separatrix> is bounded, although the radial motion is not periodic. If $v=0$, the <centrifugal barrier> disappears and the particle may pass through the axis, still with bounded $r$.

The source leaves a direction ambiguity at points where $f'(a)=0$: every horizontal tangent direction is then possible, so “projected horizontally” alone does not imply $h=av$. The displayed inequality uses the azimuthal launch specified above; at $f'(a)\ne0$ that direction is forced by horizontality and tangency.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Use the <dimensionless variables>
$$
A=\frac{a^2}{4R^2},\qquad x=\frac{r^2}{4R^2},\qquad\beta=\frac{v}{\sqrt{2gR}}.
$$
The stated $R>a/2$ gives $0<A<1$. Since $f(r)=4Rx(1-x)$, the inequality from <5/b> factorizes as
$$
\boxed{D(r)=4R^3(x-A)\left[4x^2-4(1-A)x+\beta^2\right]\geq0.}
$$
The two additional possible <turning points> are
$$
x_\pm=\frac{1-A\pm\sqrt{(1-A)^2-\beta^2}}2.
$$
They are real exactly when $\beta+A\leq1$. This exposes the intended critical value
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{v}{\sqrt{2gR}}+\frac{a^2}{4R^2}\right)_{\rm crit}=1,}
$$
but the ordering of the initial radius $A$ relative to these roots must also be checked.

First suppose $0<A<1/3$. Then $A<(1-A)/2$. If $\beta<1-A$, the allowed branch starting at $A$ is trapped either between $x_-$ and $A$ or between $A$ and $x_-$, according to its initial direction. At the coincident-root value $\beta^2=4A(1-2A)$ it is exactly a <circular orbit>. For $\beta=0$, the inner radial bound is the axis rather than a nonzero <turning point>.

At $\beta=1-A$, the additional roots coalesce at $x_*=(1-A)/2>A$. The particle starts outward and approaches $x_*$, or
$$
r_*=R\sqrt{2(1-A)},
$$
without reaching it in finite time. Indeed $D$ is proportional to $(x-A)(x-x_*)^2$ and the radial <energy> equation gives $dt/dx$ proportional to $1/|x-x_*|$ near $x_*$. Its integral diverges. This is a bounded <separatrix> approaching an unstable <circular orbit>. For $\beta>1-A$, there is no outer root and the particle escapes. Thus **for $0<A<1/3$, boundedness holds exactly when $\beta+A\leq1$**, with the equality case included. At $A=1/3$, the same inequality characterizes boundedness, but equality is the exact <circular orbit> at the starting radius.

**The source's weaker assumption $A<1$ is insufficient to make this single threshold a general criterion.** For $1/3<A<1/2$, the initial radius lies beyond the coalescing-root position. The transition is instead the unstable <circular orbit> at
$$
\beta_{\rm circ}=2\sqrt{A(1-2A)}<1-A.
$$
Below this <speed> the initial radial <acceleration> is inward and the particle remains in the inner allowed interval. At equality it stays exactly circular; above it the initial <acceleration> is outward and the accessible branch extends to infinity, even if $\beta+A<1$. For $A>1/2$, the <surface> slopes downward outward and every such launch escapes. At $A=1/2$, only $\beta=0$ remains at rest on the summit circle.

Consequently the complete classification for the intended azimuthal launch, including zero launch <speed> and exact equilibria, is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{ll}
0<A\leq1/3:&\text{bounded iff }0\leq\beta\leq1-A,\\
1/3<A\leq1/2:&\text{bounded iff }0\leq\beta\leq2\sqrt{A(1-2A)},\\
1/2<A<1:&\text{unbounded for every }\beta\geq0.
\end{array}}
$$
For a concrete counterexample to the unqualified proposed criterion, take $A=3/4$ and $\beta=1/10$. Then $\beta+A=17/20<1$, but $f'(a)<0$ and the outward motion has no accessible outer barrier. This satisfies the printed radius assumption and escapes. A sufficient additional radius restriction for the intended threshold is $a\leq2R/\sqrt3$, with the equality and <circular orbit> qualifications above.

= 6
{parent=Mathematics IV}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The uniform rod has <linear mass density> $m/\ell$. Integrating squared distance from the pivot gives its <moment of inertia> about the axis perpendicular to the plane of motion:
$$
\boxed{I_O=\int_0^\ell s^2\frac m\ell\,ds=\frac13m\ell^2.}
$$
Measure $\theta$ from the downward vertical. The <center of mass> is at distance $\ell/2$ from the pivot, so <gravity> produces the restoring <torque> $-mg(\ell/2)\sin\theta$. The fixed-axis <rotational equation of motion> is $I_O\ddot\theta=\tau_O$. Therefore the <physical pendulum> obeys
$$
\boxed{\ddot\theta=-\frac{3g}{2\ell}\sin\theta.}
$$
The pivot <force> has zero <torque> about the pivot.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The disc is rigidly attached, so its rotation axis is perpendicular to its face. Its <moment of inertia> about its center is $Ma^2/2$: with <surface mass density> $M/(\pi a^2)$, an annulus of <radius> $s$ contributes $s^2[M/(\pi a^2)]2\pi s\,ds$, whose integral is $Ma^2/2$. The <parallel axis theorem> adds $Md^2$ at the pivot. Adding the rod's <moment of inertia> gives
$$
\boxed{I_O=\frac13m\ell^2+M\left(d^2+\frac{a^2}{2}\right)
=m\ell^2\left[\frac13+\mu\left(\delta^2+\frac{\alpha^2}{2}\right)\right].}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The rod and disc contribute restoring <torques> $-mg(\ell/2)\sin\theta$ and $-Mgd\sin\theta$, respectively. Using <6/b/i> in the <rotational equation of motion> gives
$$
\boxed{\ddot\theta=-\frac g\ell\,
\frac{\frac12+\mu\delta}{\frac13+\mu(\delta^2+\alpha^2/2)}\sin\theta.}
$$
Only the disc's <center of mass> enters its gravitational <torque>; its finite <radius> enters its <moment of inertia>. The formula is also regular at $M=m$, although the source happens to exclude that value.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <small-angle approximation> $\sin\theta=\theta+O(\theta^3)$ reduces <6/b/ii> to $\ddot\theta+\omega^2\theta=0$, where
$$
\boxed{\omega=\sqrt{\frac g\ell\,
\frac{\frac12+\mu\delta}{\frac13+\mu(\delta^2+\alpha^2/2)}}.}
$$
The coefficient is positive, so the solution is <simple harmonic motion>, $\theta(t)=C\cos\omega t+D\sin\omega t$. Here $\omega$ is the <angular frequency>; the ordinary <frequency> and <period> are
$$
\boxed{f=\frac\omega{2\pi},\qquad T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

For a fixed attachment ratio $\delta>0$, making the disc much heavier than the rod gives
$$
\omega^2\longrightarrow\frac g\ell\frac{\delta}{\delta^2+\alpha^2/2}
=\frac{gd}{d^2+a^2/2}.
$$
If the disc is also small relative to the attachment distance, $\alpha/\delta=a/d\to0$, its <moment of inertia> about its own center is negligible compared with $Md^2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\omega^2\longrightarrow\frac gd,\qquad T\longrightarrow2\pi\sqrt{\frac dg}.}
$$
This is a <simple pendulum> with a <point mass> at the disc's center and effective length $d$; it is length $\ell$ only if $d=\ell$. Even before the <small-angle approximation>, its equation tends to $\ddot\theta=-(g/d)\sin\theta$.

If $\delta$ is allowed to tend to zero together with the other parameters, the bare conditions $\alpha\ll1$ and $\mu\gg1$ do not alone justify that reduction. The needed dominance conditions are $\mu\delta\gg1$, $\mu\delta^2\gg1$, and $\alpha/\delta\ll1$, so that the rod contributes negligible gravitational <torque> and <moment of inertia>, and the disc behaves as a <point mass>. They follow automatically in the combined limit with fixed $\delta>0$.

= 7
