= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <Euclidean algorithm> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
497&=259+238,\\
259&=238+21,\\
238&=11\cdot21+7,\\
21&=3\cdot7.
\end{aligned}
$$
The last nonzero remainder is the <greatest common divisor>, so $\boxed{\gcd(259,497)=7}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Back-substitution in the <Euclidean algorithm> gives the <Bézout identity>
$$
7=238-11\cdot21=12\cdot238-11\cdot259=12\cdot497-23\cdot259.
$$
Multiplying by five produces a particular solution $(x,y)=(-115,60)$. Since $259/7=37$ and $497/7=71$ are <coprime integers>, all solutions of the <linear Diophantine equation> are
$$
(x,y)=(-115+71t,\ 60-37t),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Replacing $t$ by $t+2$ gives the smaller equivalent starting solution
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(27+71t,\ -14-37t),\qquad t\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Indeed $259\cdot27-497\cdot14=35$. Conversely, subtracting this solution from any other gives $37(x-27)=-71(y+14)$. <Coprimality> forces $71$ to divide $x-27$, and then $y+14$ is the corresponding multiple of $-37$. This proves completeness of the family.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Define the relative reciprocal error $\varepsilon_k=1-px_k$. The iteration has the exact identity
$$
px_{k+1}=3px_k-3(px_k)^2+(px_k)^3=1-(1-px_k)^3,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\varepsilon_{k+1}=\varepsilon_k^3,\qquad\varepsilon_k=\varepsilon_0^{3^k}.}
$$
Consequently $|1-px_0|<1$ guarantees $x_k\to1/p$. For real $p,x_0$, this condition is $0<px_0<2$. A starting guess can be found without division by choosing $x_0$ with the sign of $p$ and repeatedly halving its magnitude until $0<px_0<2$.

For the absolute signed error $e_k=x_k-1/p$, the same identity gives
$$
\boxed{e_{k+1}=p^2e_k^3.}
$$
Thus the method has <cubic convergence>, of <order of convergence> three, with asymptotic error constant $p^2>0$ for a nonexact convergent start. Convergence requires a suitable guess: $px_0=0$ and $px_0=2$ correspond to $\varepsilon_0=1$ and $\varepsilon_0=-1$, respectively, so both are fixed failures. For $|1-px_0|>1$ the error magnitude grows without bound. The exact initial value $px_0=1$ is already the answer.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For a twice <continuously differentiable function> $F$, its <gradient> and <Hessian matrix> are
$$
\nabla F(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}F_x\\F_y\end{pmatrix},\qquad
H_F(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}F_{xx}&F_{xy}\\F_{yx}&F_{yy}\end{pmatrix}.
$$
A <critical point> solves $\nabla F=0$. The <Jacobian matrix> of this vector equation is $H_F$, so the <multivariable Newton iteration> is
$$
\boxed{H_F(\mathbf x_k)\mathbf s_k=-\nabla F(\mathbf x_k),\qquad\mathbf x_{k+1}=\mathbf x_k+\mathbf s_k.}
$$
Equivalently $\mathbf x_{k+1}=\mathbf x_k-H_F(\mathbf x_k)^{-1}\nabla F(\mathbf x_k)$ when the <Hessian matrix> is invertible. Solving the <linear system> is the direct computational form. A <critical point> found in this way need not be a <local minimum>; its type requires a separate check.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <gradient> and constant <Hessian matrix> are
$$
\nabla F=\begin{pmatrix}6x-4y+4\\4y-4x-4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
H_F=\begin{pmatrix}6&-4\\-4&4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
H_F^{-1}=\frac18\begin{pmatrix}4&4\\4&6\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At $\mathbf x_0=(1,0)^T$, $\nabla F=(10,-8)^T$ and $H_F^{-1}\nabla F=(1,-1)^T$. Hence one <multivariable Newton iteration> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x_1=(1,0)^T-(1,-1)^T=(0,1)^T.}
$$
At this point the <gradient> vanishes, so every subsequent iterate is $(0,1)$. The one-step result follows because the <gradient> of a quadratic <polynomial> is affine and its linearization is exact. Since the leading principal minors of $H_F$ are $6>0$ and $8>0$, it is a <positive-definite matrix>. Alternatively,
$$
F(x,y)=x^2+2(y-x-1)^2-1
$$
directly proves that **$(0,1)$ is the unique <global minimum>, with value $-1$**.
